{"qid":"Q1841483","names":["Dutch Ceylon"],"title":"Dutch Ceylon","endonym":null,"capsule":"Dutch Ceylon was a trading-empire governorate of the Dutch East India Company (VOC) covering most of Sri Lanka's coastline from 1658, when Colombo fell to a joint Dutch-Kandyan assault that expelled the Portuguese. The VOC monopolised cinnamon, elephants, and other export commodities, pursuing a policy of coerced labour and caste-based plantation slavery particularly in the Jaffna peninsula. The interior Kingdom of Kandy was never subjugated, limiting Dutch control to a coastal perimeter; when British forces—assisted by the defection of the Swiss mercenary De Meuron Regiment—occupied Colombo in February 1796, the VOC's Sri Lankan possessions passed to the British Crown without significant resistance.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The coastal footprint of Dutch Ceylon is well-documented through VOC administrative records; island-wide extent is clearly bounded by the un-conquered Kandyan interior.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Dutch Ceylon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dutch_Ceylon"},{"label":"Britannica: Dutch East India Company","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Dutch-East-India-Company"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Kandy","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Kandy"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Dutch Ceylon","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Laurens Pijl","nativeName":"Laurens Pijl","roman":"","start":1679,"end":1692,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Thomas van Rhee","nativeName":"Thomas van Rhee","roman":"","start